  "live_records": {
    "q-52485": {
      "dom_id": "step-q52485",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-4(x+1)(x-2)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-4<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-1+2}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4(x+1)(x-2) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{1}{2}+1)(\\frac{1}{2}-2) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(\\frac{1}{2},9),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,9].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14253498/assets/b45df69ab8780eabce5b57a30cf7448a011361e18e28989249e1eca439b17028.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
