    "q-90973": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90973",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "What is the range of the function $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-16x+8?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=4>0,$ $b=-16,$ and $c=8.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-16)}{2\\cdot 4}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=2$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4{x}^{2}-16x+8 \\\\  & =4(2)^{2}-16(2)+8 \\\\  & =16-32+8 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(2,-8),$ and the range of the function is $[-8,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14253498/assets/ef95a03e21bf6d12fec48da87ded1230ccb124cbed480910f883520aa30a8db7.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
