    "q-154352": {
      "dom_id": "step-q154352",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{12}(x-5)(x+7)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-\\frac{1}{12}<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-7$ and ${x}_{2}=5,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-7+5}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=-1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{12}(x-5)(x+7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(-1-5)(-1+7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-6)\\cdot (6) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-1,3),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,3].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14253498/assets/f2b32570242760c89292a1c19ff005b5e750c04e9e974775d2c05d42947b95d6.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
